/*  sbend_gap.in -- SBEND scraping on HGAP (vertical) and HAPERT (horizontal).

    Element under test:
      ELEM: SBEND, L = 0.1 m, ANGLE = 0, HGAP = 0.005, HAPERT = 0.010
    HGAP is the half pole gap and sets the VERTICAL half-aperture (5 mm). HAPERT is
    the half-aperture in the bend plane and sets the HORIZONTAL one (10 mm). Both are
    half-widths, unlike the APERTURE string, whose arguments are full widths.

    ANGLE = 0 on purpose: the bend then carries no field, so this deck measures the
    aperture and nothing else. x and y are exactly invariant along the line and the
    beam size at the aperture equals the launched size. (HGAP > 0 still switches on the
    Enge fringe profile, but it scales a zero field, and the edge-focusing kick needs a
    non-zero curvature, so neither moves a particle here.)

    Beam: sigma_x = 5 mm, sigma_y = 2.5 mm, positions truncated at the GAUSS default
    3 sigma (CUTOFFX / CUTOFFY / CUTOFFLONG), zero momentum spread.

    Both half-apertures are 2 sigma in their own plane, and a rectangle is separable:

      transmission = ( erf(2/sqrt(2)) / erf(3/sqrt(2)) )^2 = 0.916009

      -> 9160 +- 28 of 10000 particles survive.

    The two failure modes this separates:
      - 9571 means the vertical cut came from HAPERT (the old square aperture), i.e.
        y was cut at 10 mm = 4 sigma, outside the 3 sigma truncation, so not at all.
        The 411 particle gap is ~15 sigma of the sampling noise.
      - 10000 means no aperture was installed on the bend at all.

    Compare with drift_rectangle, which reaches the same 0.916009 through
    APERTURE = "RECTANGLE(0.02, 0.01)" on a DRIFT.
*/

OPTION, PSDUMPFREQ    = 200;
OPTION, STATDUMPFREQ  = 5;
OPTION, BOUNDPDESTROY = 1000000;   // the N-sigma outlier cut must never fire:
                                   // the aperture must be the only source of loss
Option, VERSION = 10900;

Title, string="sbend_gap";

REAL Edes  = 0.1;                          // kinetic energy [GeV]
REAL gamma = (Edes + EMASS) / EMASS;
REAL beta  = sqrt(1 - (1 / gamma^2));
REAL P0    = gamma * beta * EMASS;         // GeV/c; also sets the OpalData field reference

// The monitors sit 0.1 m clear of the element body so their +-5 mm selection
// window never overlaps the aperture marking window [0.5, 0.6).
D_IN:    DRIFT,   L = 0.5, ELEMEDGE = 0.0;
MON_IN:  MONITOR, ELEMEDGE = 0.4, DELETEONTRANSVERSEEXIT = FALSE;
ELEM:    SBEND,   L = 0.1, ELEMEDGE = 0.5,
         ANGLE = 0.0, HGAP = 0.005, HAPERT = 0.010;
MON_OUT: MONITOR, ELEMEDGE = 0.7, DELETEONTRANSVERSEEXIT = FALSE;
D_OUT:   DRIFT,   L = 0.5, ELEMEDGE = 0.6;

ApLine: Line = (D_IN, MON_IN, ELEM, MON_OUT, D_OUT);

FS1: Fieldsolver, TYPE = NONE,
     NX = 16, NY = 16, NZ = 16,
     PARFFTX = true, PARFFTY = true, PARFFTZ = true,
     BCFFTX = open, BCFFTY = open, BCFFTZ = open,
     BBOXINCR = 1, GREENSF = INTEGRATED;

REAL n_particles = 10000;
REAL PC_beam     = P0;                     // bunch mean momentum, on design

Dist: DISTRIBUTION, TYPE = GAUSS,
      SIGMAX  = 0.005,  SIGMAPX = 0.0,
      SIGMAY  = 0.0025, SIGMAPY = 0.0,
      SIGMAZ  = 0.0005, SIGMAPZ = 0.0,
      NPARTDIST = n_particles;
ES1: EMISSIONSOURCE, DISTRIBUTION = Dist;
mySources: EMISSIONSOURCELIST = (ES1);
BEAM1: BEAM, PARTICLE = ELECTRON, PC = PC_beam, NALLOC = n_particles,
       BCHARGE = 1.6e-15, SOURCES = mySources, CHARGE = -1;

TRACK, LINE = ApLine, BEAM = BEAM1,
       MAXSTEPS = 2000000, DT = 1e-12, ZSTOP = 1;
RUN, METHOD = "PARALLEL", FIELDSOLVER = FS1;
ENDTRACK;
Quit;
